## 1. Introduction
With the surging demand of clean and renewable energy to meet sustainable development goals, massive efforts have been put into the development of electrochemical energy conversion and storage devices, including fuel cells, metal–air batteries, and water electrolyzers.[1] To secure the productivity and efficiency of these devices, active electrocatalysts become highly demanding nowadays.[2] Noble-metal-based electrocatalysts are still the best candidates to catalyze these reactions, but their scarcity and prohibitive price have provoked the production of electrocatalysts based on non-noble metals.[3] Notably, multifunctional electrocatalysts become increasingly important in various practical electrochemical energy conversion devices, such as rechargeable metal–air batteries, which require electrocatalysts to satisfactorily catalyze both oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER).[4] Apart from the competent performance of noblemetal-free electrocatalysts, the potential for device deployment and scalability is also inseparable.[1b,5] Therefore, it is fascinating to explore efficient single and multifunctional electrocatalysts with simplified fabrication processes and a substantial potential for reducing the cost of electrochemical devices.[6]
Because various electrochemical reactions proceed via distinct reaction pathways, the development of single and multifunctional electrocatalysts are still challenging and under intensive study.[6b,7] Especially for rechargeable metal–air batteries, OER and ORR occurred on air cathode are repeatedly switched during charging and discharging processes.[8] Nowadays, the durability of electrocatalysts with single catalytically active components remains a critical issue, and only a few single-component electrocatalysts show satisfactory bifunctional performance because single active component is particularly vulnerable during the switched reaction process.[1a,6d,9] In this regard, combining multiple active components together to accelerate their sluggish kinetics affords opportunities to obtain promising bifunctional oxygen electrocatalysts.[4b,c,10] Heteroatoms doped carbon materials, which can serve as the conducive scaffolds, draw significant attention since the easily constructed porous structures offer plentiful accessible active sites for ORR and enhance the mass/charge transfer ability from active sites to the electrode surface.[4d,5d,11] Meanwhile, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which intensively reconstruct into metal hydroxide with surface-adsorbed ligands, exhibit excellent OER performance superior to the corresponding metal hydroxide.[12] It is also demonstrated that the adsorbed ligands could satisfactorily enhance the electrocatalytic activity of metal hydroxides toward OER by modulating their electronic structure.[13] Though MOFs are promising electrocatalysts for OER, most MOFs seriously suffer from inherently low electrical conductivity.[14] One appealing and fruitful strategy to improve the catalytic activity for OER is to integrate the MOFs with highly conductive substrates, such as carbon nanofiber, carbon nanotubes, and graphene.[15] Accordingly, rational architecture designs of integrating carbon-based, ORRactive components with MOF-based, OER-active components would lead to efficient exposure of both active sites, fast electron and mass transport, and synergistic effects between each component, which are desirable for the exceptional bifunctional electrocatalysts. However, uniformly growing MOFs on conductive supports are with many challenges and ineffective interfacial contacts between different active components would induce a large catalytic performance compromise.[15d,16] Thus, feasible fabrication strategy should focus on securing the rational distribution of these two catalytic active components in the composites.
Here, theoretical studies revealed that benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) ligand could accelerate OER kinetics of NiFe-layered double hydroxide (NiFe-LDH) by optimizing the binding energies of intermediates. Experimentally, we came up with a facile strategy to prepare amorphous bimetallic NiFe-MOFs nanosheets with superior OER activity. Furthermore, highly active bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst was successfully designed by in situ wrapping NiFe-MOFs on heteroatomsdoped porous carbon spheres with excellent ORR activity (NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs). With the robust core–shell morphology and intimate interaction between NiFe-MOFs and porous carbon spheres, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs achieve remarkable performance toward bifunctional electrocatalysis. Interestingly, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs show exceptional low potential gap $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{A}E}=0.61\:\mathrm{V})$ in $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}}$ KOH, which is the difference between the potential to reach an OER current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ $(E_{10})$ and ORR half-wave potential $\left(E_{1/2}\right)$ . When used as air cathode of aqueous rechargeable zinc–air batteries (ZABs), it can be cycled for $450\mathrm{h}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ without obvious degradation. Also, the quasi-solid-state ZAB shows excellent flexibility and cycling stability.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The composite electrocatalyst for bifunctional oxygen catalysis was thus rationally fabricated by integrating heteroatoms-doped carbon with NiFe-MOFs, which serve as active components toward ORR and OER, respectively. Typical twostep process for synthesizing the composite electrocatalyst is shown in Figure 2a. First, well-defined activated carbon spheres (ACSs), which serve as the ORR active component and conductive support, were thermal treated with N- and P-containing precursors to produce N- and P-codoped ACSs (NP-ACSs).[19] Second, flexible amorphous NiFe-MOFs nanosheets, as excellent OER active components, were in situ assembled onto
NP-ACSs to yield the composite electrocatalyst (denoted as NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs).
The morphologies of electrocatalysts were identified by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images. As shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information), NP-ACSs possess a well-defined spherical morphology and are separately distributed. Meanwhile, amorphous NiFe-MOFs exhibit a flexible nanosheets structure (Figure S3, Supporting Information). In contrast, the originally isolated NP-ACSs are intertwined together with NiFe-MOFs, and NP-ACSs@NiFe-MOFs exhibit rough surface with the presence of NiFe-MOFs nanosheets wrapping on NP-ACSs (Figure  2b). Moreover, a certain number of NiFe-MOFs nanosheets can be observed as linkers and combine the neighboring NP-ACSs together. Preferential assembly of NiFe-MOFs on NP-ACSs is also confirmed by TEM images in Figure  2c and Figure S4 (Supporting Information), revealing that free-standing nanosheets can be barely observed and numerous fragments of nanosheets are present on NP-ACSs. Meanwhile, the size of nanosheets in NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs is smaller than that in pristine NiFe-MOFs. Therefore, it is proposed that a heterogeneous nucleation process is favorable for the growth of NiFe-MOFs when NP-ACSs is introduced, which secures the close contact between MOFs and NP-ACSs. The high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HR-TEM) images in Figure  2d,e also demonstrate the intimate interfacial interaction between the amorphous NiFe-MOFs and NP-ACSs with crystallized graphitic carbon layers, supporting a preferable heterogeneous nucleation of NiFe-MOFs.
Scanning transmission electron microscopy and energy dispersive X-ray elemental mappings of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs (Figure  2f) illustrate that C, N, and P elements are uniformly distributed throughout the whole carbon support, which is analogous to NP-ACSs (Figure S5, Supporting Information) and indicates the homogeneous doping of these heteroatoms. Meanwhile, O, Ni, and Fe element, which is primarily from the NiFe-MOFs (Figure S6, Supporting Information), can be distinguished not only on NP-ACSs, but also from the boundaries of NP-ACSs. These experimental results support the in situ growth and assembly of NiFe-MOFs nanosheets on the surface of NP-ACSs, and the specific core–shell architecture is anticipated to minimize the electron transport distance of the NiFe-MOFs, which contribute to the enhancement of OER performance. In addition, NP-ACSs@NiFe-MOFs possess a substantial Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $833\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ according to the nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms (Figure S7, Supporting Information), which is slightly smaller than that of NP-ACSs (Figure S8, Supporting Information) and dramatically larger than the NiFe-MOFs (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Both samples also own an analogous pore size distribution, revealing that the assembly of MOFs did not change the pore structure of NP-ACSs. These results ambiguously disclose that NiFe-MOFs predominantly grow on the surface of NP-ACSs. The retained porous architecture can effectively enhance accessibility of the active sites in NP-ACSs for ORR, and the intimate contact between NiFe-MOFs and conductive carbon support secures an excellent electrical conductivity of the active sites for OER. Therefore, the composite electrocatalyst is expected to afford excellent performance toward ORR and OER.
As shown by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) pattern in Figure 3a, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs have a distinguished diffraction peak centered at $24^{\circ}$ , which is simar to NP-ACSs (Figure S10, Supporting Information) and can be ascribed to graphitic carbon and amorphous NiFe-MOFs.[20] Notably, there is no other observed peaks, which correspond to the
HR-TEM results and demonstrate the amorphous nature of the NiFe-MOFs nanosheets.[20b,21] In addition, according to the Raman spectroscopy (Figure S11, Supporting Information), the intensity ratio between D band and G band $\left(I_{\mathrm{D}}{:}I_{\mathrm{G}}\right)$ for NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs and NP-ACSs is similar, demonstrating that the structure of carbon matrix did not change after the wrapping of NiFe-MOFs.
The electrocatalytic OER performance of electrocatalysts were assessed in $1\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{KOH}$ , and the polarization curves recorded using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) technique are displayed in Figure 4a. Particularly, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs show the lowest potential of 1.46  V to reach 10  mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $(E_{10}=1.46\mathrm{~V~}_{\sun}$ ) among all these samples (Figure  4b). In contrast, NP-ACSs yield negligible OER current in the applied potential range, and NiFe-MOFs show inferior OER activity to NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs. Therefore, NiFe-MOFs are confirmed as the predominant active component toward OER. Meanwhile, NP-ACSs promote the distribution and increase the electrical conductivity of NiFe-MOFs, which contribute to the superior OER performance of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs. Tafel slopes were then calculated to identify reaction kinetics of the electrocatalysts. As shown in Figure  4c, Tafel slope for NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs is $45.8~\mathrm{\mV}$ dec−1, which is lower than NiFe-MOFs $(54.8\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1})}$ , NP-ACSs $(78.5~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ , and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(51.4~\mathrm{~mV}~\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ , indicating the facilitated OER kinetics of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs.[28] These results demonstrate the key role of NiFe-MOFs and carbon support in synergistically boosting OER performance. OER mechanism of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs was further investigated using rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) strategy.[12b,29] As shown in Figure  4d, current density of OER on disk electrode increases simultaneously with the rise of applied potential, but negligible current density signifying the yield of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be observed on
The superior OER performance of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs is also highlighted by the results obtained in $0.1\textbf{M}$ KOH. As shown in Figure  4g,h, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs possess significantly smaller $E_{10}$ (1.48  V) and Tafel slope $(50.6~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ than those for ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ benchmark, which are $1.63\mathrm{~V~}$ and $68.3~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , respectively. Here, 3D carbon spheres, which support the NiFe-MOFs are expected to benefit the exposure of active species.[6a,15d,30] Since the precise number of actual reaction active sites is hard to be determined, electrochemical active surface area was applied to ambiguously reveal the exposed reaction areas of the catalysts.[6c,12h,31] Cyclic voltammetry (CV) experiments (Figure S16, Supporting Information) were conducted at various scan rates to calculate the double layer capacity $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ . As shown in Figure  4i, NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs have a significantly larger $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of $36.00~\mathrm{~mF~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ than NiFe-MOFs $(0.09\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), and the large surface area of ACSs would promote the exposure of NiFe-MOFs for surface catalytic reactions. Therefore, the remarkable OER performance of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs can be attributed to the synergistic effect between MOF-derived BDC-adsorbed $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ Fe-LDH with high intrinsic activity and conductive carbon support. Specifically, the unique core–shell structure is expected to secure the exposure of OER active component, and the intimate interaction between MOFs and carbon support can accelerate the electron transfer for OER.[2b,15c,32]
The electrocatalytic ORR activity of the electrocatalysts was systematically evaluated in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}$ KOH. NiFe-MOFs, NP-ACSs, and benchmark $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ were also tested for comparison.
Tafel slope was calculated. As shown by Tafel plots (Figure 5b) derived from the corresponding LSV curves, the Tafel slope for NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs is $59.3~\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which is similar to NP-ACSs $(59.4~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(55.8\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}})$ , and much smaller than NiFe-MOFs $(112~\mathrm{~mV~}\mathrm{~dec^{-1}})$ . The smaller Tafel slope further certifies a faster ORR kinetics on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs and NP-ACSs electrode as $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ [33] The good linearity of Koutecký–Levich (K–L) plots, which are derived from LSV curves under different rotating rates, demonstrate that ORR on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs follow first order reaction kinetics (Figure 5c).[34] Moreover, the electron transfer number (n) of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs was calculated to be around four, evidencing the efficient four-electron ORR pathway. n and hydrogen peroxide yield $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\%)$ of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ were also calculated from the LSV curves measured by RRDE strategy (Figure S18, Supporting Information) in the potential range of $0.2\mathrm{-}1.0\mathrm{V}.$ As shown in Figure 5d, the $H_{2}O_{2}\%$ of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs is $<2.6\%$ , and $n$ is near to 4.0 in the applied potential range, supporting a highly efficient fourelectron ORR process on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs.
As revealed by the LSV curves before and after ADT (Figure S19, Supporting Information), NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs exhibit exceptional durability without noticeable shift of polarization curve after $5000\mathrm{CV}$ cycles. The superb long-term stability is also evidenced by the chronoamperometry test (Figure  5e), showing that the current density for NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs declined slightly to $97.8\%$ after $50~000~\mathrm{~s~}$ . In contrast, Pt/C shows an obvious drop of $14.4\%$ in current density. The methanol tolerance property of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs was evaluated and compared with $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Figure S20, Supporting Information).
After the injection of methanol, no obvious drop of current density is observed for NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs, while the current density of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ decreased sharply, elucidating the superior methanol tolerance property of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs. Moreover, the robustness of NP-ACSs@NiFe-MOFs was evaluated by examining the electrocatalyst after long-term stability test. The XRD pattern, Raman spectrum, SEM image, and TEM image of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs after stability test (Figures S21 and S22, Supporting Information) demonstrate no noticeable morphology and structure change during the test, illustrating that the as-constructed electrocatalyst has outstanding morphological stability and corrosion resistance. Meanwhile, the elemental distributions (Figure S23, Supporting Information) of Fe, N, and P elements after the ADT further demonstrate the exceptional stability of the core–shell structure. Altogether, results confirmed that NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs after the ADT process still preserved the pristine crystal phase, morphology structure, and composition, further elucidating their outstanding structural stability. Therefore, the superior ORR activity and stability can be primarily assigned to NP-ACSs with plentiful active sites and unique architecture. In particular, the plentiful edge-hosted N and $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ species serve as active sites with excellent intrinsic activity toward ORR. Meanwhile, the rich pores not only accelerate the mass transfer, but also facilitate the exposure of active sites during ORR.
Bifunctional activity for oxygen electrocatalysis was then assessed by calculating the voltage gap $(\Delta E)$ between $E_{10}$ and $E_{1/2}$ $(\Delta E=E_{10}–E_{1/2})$ recorded in $0.1\textbf{M}$ KOH. As shown in Figure 5f,
NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs exhibit an obviously smaller $\Delta E$ of $0.61\mathrm{V}$ than the counterparts of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $\Delta E=0.76\mathrm{~V~}$ ), and this demonstrates its remarkable activity toward bifunctional oxygen electrocatalysis. The exceptionally smaller $\Delta E$ secures NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs as a promising alternative to noble-metal-based bifunctional electrocatalysts for efficient energy conversion devices, such as rechargeable metal–air batteries.
AqueousZAB using NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs as air cathode was assembled as shown in Figure 6a, and the ZAB based on commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}{+}\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ mixture was tested for comparison. The charge and discharge curves in Figure 6b show that ZAB based on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs yields a higher discharge voltage, a lower charge voltage, and a smaller charge–discharge voltage gap than the one assembled using $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{+RuO}_{2}$ , suggesting its exceptionally high cycling efficiency. Moreover, as shown in Figure  6c, ZAB based on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs delivered a peak power density as high as $255\mathrm{~mW~cm^{-2}}$ , 1.6 times higher than that for ZAB based on $\mathrm{Pt/C}{+}\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(156~\mathrm{mw}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ . Galvanostatic discharge plots at different current densities in Figure  6d further demonstrates that ZAB based on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs presents higher discharge voltages than $\mathrm{Pt/C}{+}\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ -based one when discharged at current densities from 2 to $50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . Importantly, galvanostatic cycling durability and rechargeability are important to demonstrate the practical applications of ZAB. Notably, as shown in Figure  6e, when cycled at a constant current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , ZAB driven by NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs displays a small charge/ discharge voltage gap of 0.77  V after cycling for $50\mathrm{~h~}$ (1  h per cycle). After the long cycling test for $450\ \mathrm{h},$ , the voltage gap experienced a slight increase to $0.79{\mathrm{~V}},$ highlighting a negligible performance deterioration. However, the one catalyzed by $\mathrm{Pt/C}{+}\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ suffers obvious activity decay within $40\mathrm{h}$ (Figure S24, Supporting Information). Notably, the ZAB assembled with NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs exhibits lower charge/discharge voltage gap and better cycling stability than those of the reported works (Table S3, Supporting Information). To reveal its potential application in wearable devices, flexible ZAB was constructed as shown in Figure  6g. Flexible ZAB based on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs exhibits higher open circuit potential and discharge power density than $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}{+}\mathrm{Ru}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -based one (Figure S25, Supporting Information). Importantly, the cycling performance shown in Figure  6h demonstrates the flexible ZAB based on NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs yields smaller charge voltages and smaller charge/discharge voltage gaps than $\mathrm{Pt/C}{+}\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ -based one. The mechanical flexibility of flexible ZAB was evaluated by folding the ZAB into different bending angles. As revealed in Figure 6i, when flexible ZAB was bended at $0^{\circ}$ , $45^{\circ}$ . $90^{\circ}$ and $145^{\circ}$ , the charge and discharge voltages remained stable, demonstrating the superior flexibility. Therefore, the remarkable bifunctional electrocatalysis activity of NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs significantly contributed to the satisfactory performance of the aqueous and flexible ZABs.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we demonstrated a rational strategy to prepare excellent electrocatalysts for bifunctional oxygen electrocatalysis by successfully integrating efficient OER and ORR active components together. The intrinsically high OER activity of NiFe-MOFs, enhanced electron transfer by the tightly enwrapped conductive NP-ACSs, and abundant exposed active sites inherited from the highly porous architecture synergistically contribute to the exceptional bifunctional activity. NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs feature satisfactory ORR and superior OER performances with notably small potential gap $\begin{array}{r}{(A E=0.61\mathrm{~V~}}\end{array}$ ) in $0.1\textbf{M}$ KOH and excellent long-term stability. The aqueous ZAB built by NP-ACSs $@$ NiFe-MOFs displays high power density, super cycle stability at large current density. The assembled flexible ZAB also shows excellent long-term durability and encouraging flexibility. This work highlights a novel strategy to efficiently integrate different catalytic components, which can be used to prepare strongly competitive multifunctional electrocatalysts to realize the widespread application of electrochemical energy conversion devices.
